## DESCRIPTION
## Find the equation of all points perpendicular to a vector in R^3.
## ENDDESCRIPTION
## KEYWORDS('plane','equation')

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Inner products)
## DBsection(Computing with dot products)
## Date(September 5, 2011)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',        'PGML.pl',
    'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'parserImplicitPlane.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('ImplicitPlane');
Context()->variables->are(x => 'Real', y => 'Real', z => 'Real');

$u = ColumnVector(non_zero_point3D(-5, 5));
$x = non_zero_point3D(-5, 5);

$ans = ImplicitPlane([ $u->value ], $u . $x, [ 'x', 'y', 'z' ]);

BEGIN_PGML

Consider the vector [`` \vec{u} = [$u] ``]

The set of points that are perpendicular to [` \vec{u} `] and pass through
the point [`[$x]`] form an equation.

The equation is [___]{$ans}

Hint: think geometrically about this problem.  What is the geometric object
that is perpendicular to a vector?  How best write it down as a function of
[`x, y`] and [`z`]?
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The set of points is a plane.  The given vector will be the normal vector of the plane.
Therefore, to find the equation
[``` [$u] \cdot \langle x-[@ ($x->value)[0] @],y-[@ ($x->value)[1] @],
x-[@ ($x->value)[2] @] \rangle =0 ```]
or
[```[$ans1]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
